<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Hindu Shahis</h1><p class="st-meta">822 CE – 1026 CE (approx.) · capital Udabhandapura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last Hindu dynasty of Kabul and Gandhara, which held the north-western gateway of India until Mahmud of Ghazni's invasions.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 822 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kallar">Kallar</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> minister of the last <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turk-shahis">Turk Shahis</a>, overthrew his master and founded a new dynasty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Shahis ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and, after its loss to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> around 870, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udabhandapura">Udabhandapura</a> (Hund) on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Shahis ruled a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist population from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> valley to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> 'bull and horseman' coins, showing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva's</a> bull <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nandi-hinduism">Nandi</a> and a mounted warrior, were imitated widely across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. They maintained alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and built temples in the Salt Range, and al-Biruni later praised their noble conduct.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a> launched repeated invasions: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapala">Jayapala</a> was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabuktigin">Sabuktigin</a> and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> in 1001 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, after which he committed suicide. His successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anandapala">Anandapala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trilochanapala-shahi-dynasty">Trilochanapala</a> lost further battles, and the dynasty ended by 1026 with the last Shahi kings in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hindu-shahis.jpg" alt="Hindu Shahis" width="320" height="168"><figcaption>Image: Uploadalt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coins_of_the_Shahis_8th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>822 CE – 1026 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Udabhandapura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kallar, Jayapala, Anandapala</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kabul Shahi, Hindu Shahi dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=924&amp;focus=hindu-shahis" aria-label="Hindu Shahis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6462 1109 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hindu Shahis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6757 1183h0M6748 1217h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6782 1365h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6782 1365h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Pakistan. <a href="/atlas?year=924&amp;focus=hindu-shahis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
